# install.sh on a machine that has never seen this repository -- 2026-10-02

Reproduce: `scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh`
Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, Docker 29.8.1, container image `debian:bookworm-slim`.
Commit under test: the working tree whose `scripts/install.sh` is byte-identical to
`scripts/install.sh` at `3ceac9b1` (verified with `cmp` against `git show
3ceac9b1:scripts/install.sh`).
Scope: whether the command the README puts on its front page installs a verified
binary on a clean Linux machine, and whether the two integrity checks it claims to
perform are actually performed.

## Why this was not already known

The installers had only been checked by
`scripts/ci/test-installer-signature-policy.py`, which reads the script as text and
asserts that certain error strings exist in it. Text assertions cannot tell you that
the function containing them is never called, and one of them was not.

`scripts/install.sh` defined `verify_checksum()` -- fetch `SHA256SUMS`, find the entry
for the asset, compare, exit 1 on mismatch -- and had no call site for it. Before the
fix, `git show 73a9d7c8:scripts/install.sh | grep -n 'verify_checksum\|verify_signature'`
printed:

    478:verify_checksum() {          <- definition
    521:verify_signature() {
    585:verify_signature             <- the only integrity check that runs

so `curl -fsSL .../install.sh | bash` downloaded the ~160 MB artifact, printed nothing
about the published digest, and installed. Every error path inside the function
(`could not fetch SHA256SUMS`, `SHA256SUMS has no entry for`, `checksum mismatch`, and
the `checksum OK` success line) was unreachable, and the comment above it said the
opposite of the truth. `install.ps1` and `install.bat` do the same work inline, so
only the sh installer was affected.

## Run 1: what the clean-machine run reported

`scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh` installs into a stock `debian:bookworm-slim`
container whose only added packages are `curl`, `python3`, `python3-cryptography` --
no cargo, no repository, no prior `~/.chaos`, and no `CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` passed
in. Two checks failed:

    FAILED no 'checksum OK' line: the published SHA256SUMS was not consulted
    FAILED a blank key did not fail fast (exit 0); output was: Chaos installer ...

The first is the defect above. The second was a bug in the harness, and it mattered
more than it looks:

    install_out=$(in_container sh -c "CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY=\"\"; bash ... ")

`VAR=x; cmd` assigns a shell variable and then runs the command *without* it, so
neither fail-closed control ever reached the installer. The blank-key run used the
built-in key and exited 0; the foreign-key control, which was next in line and had not
run yet, would have done the same -- i.e. the two checks that exist to prove the
signature line is not vacuous were themselves vacuous. `run_install` now goes through
`env` (`env CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY= bash ...`), and the script refuses to continue
until a probe confirms the controls can reach the child's environment:

    ok  the controls really do set CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY in the child's environment

## Fixes

1. `verify_checksum` is called before `verify_signature`, both before `chmod +x`.
2. `scripts/ci/test-installer-signature-policy.py` asserts structurally that a
   definition is not enough: each of `verify_checksum` / `verify_signature` must appear
   exactly once as a top-level call, in that order, and before `chmod +x "$TMP"`.
   Mutation-verified -- deleting the call line makes the test fail with
   `verify_checksum must be called exactly once at the top level (found 0 call sites)`,
   and restoring it makes the suite pass (7 tests).

