       ok  the controls really do set CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY in the child's environment
       ok  a blank key is refused before any download starts
       ok  a valid-but-foreign key is refused with 'signature verification FAILED'
       ok  the refused install left the working artifact untouched (0ee7d6ee3c92159d2b013c7d081cf2cc265d2d59a808ebb0fb9f2554a7b4bfa2)

    all 19 check(s) passed

Two details worth keeping. The foreign-key run prints `checksum OK` and *then*
refuses, which is the intended order -- the digest catches a corrupt or swapped
transfer, the signature is what establishes provenance, and neither is load-bearing
alone. And the digest above is the same `0ee7d6ee…a7b4bfa2` that
`scripts/verify-release-signature.sh --tag v0.4.2` recomputed independently from the
release page (see `release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log`), so the installer and that
script are looking at the same bytes.

## Cross-checks after the run

- `cmp` says the script that ran in the container is identical to
  `scripts/install.sh` at `3ceac9b1`, so the transcript describes committed content.
- `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/main/scripts/install.sh`
  is now byte-identical to the working tree. During run 1 it was not (that run printed
  a note about it), because `main` still had the version without the checksum call:
  the one-liner a reader copies from the README was behind the fix until `3ceac9b1`
  was pushed.
- The `built-in key is the repository's CHAOS_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` check reads the
  Actions variable with `gh variable get` and compares it to
  `DEFAULT_SIGNING_PUBLIC_KEY` in the script under test, so the key the installer ships
  and the key the release signs against cannot drift apart silently.

## What this file is NOT evidence about

- `install.ps1` and `install.bat`. Nothing has executed either one. Separately found
  in this round: `install.ps1` did not even parse (`The Try statement is missing its
  Catch or Finally block`) since `21f5a186`; fixed in `4d3eb266` and now gated by
  `scripts/ci/check-powershell-syntax.py`. Parsing is not installing.
- macOS, or Linux on arm64. The container was x86_64 Debian and picked
  `chaos-linux-x64`.
- The mirror paths. The run used the origin URL; `CHAOS_GITHUB_MIRROR`, `CHAOS_CN=1`
  and the public fallback list were not exercised.
- The escape hatches. `CHAOS_SKIP_CHECKSUM=1` and `CHAOS_SKIP_SIGNATURE=1` were not
  exercised -- the run asserts the opposite, that no skip line appeared.
- The upgrade path over an existing install of a *different* version, or a
  pre-existing `~/.chaos` written by an older release. The container started clean and
  the second run was the same version, i.e. a no-op.
- That `chaos update` afterwards verifies and installs a newer release. That is
  `docs/verification/updater-feed-e2e-2026-10-02.log` (loopback feed) and
  `docs/verification/release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log` (real bytes, real key),
  each with its own limits.
- Version currency: `v0.4.2` is the newest published release; the repository is ahead
  of it. Re-run against the new tag after the next release.


