## Re-run 2026-10-03: 19/19 again, and this time under a ceiling the harness owns

`bash scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh` was run again from a tree whose `scripts/install.sh` is
byte-identical to `scripts/install.sh` at `1487aded` (proved with `cmp`), and whose published copy at
`https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/main/scripts/install.sh` is byte-identical to
that same file (`cmp` against a `curl -fsSL` of the URL, and the lab performs the same comparison
itself as one of its 19 checks).

**This is no longer the script the sections above tested.** The header of this file names `3ceac9b1`
as the commit under test; that is now two changes behind. `download_github()` gained a
`min_bytes` floor (`3cd03151`, then `88511413`): a released artifact is over 150 MB, so a `200` whose
body is smaller than the floor is treated as a truncated transfer or a proxy body, the candidate is
removed, and the reason is carried into the final error. Before that, a short 200 reached the checksum
step and failed there, pointing at the digest for what was a transport problem. A run of this lab is
only worth keeping if it says *which* installer it ran, which is why the commit is named above rather
than assumed from the previous section.

**Why a re-run was owed at all.** The 2026-10-02 run of this lab died inside the harness: every Docker
lab in the repository kept its container alive with `--entrypoint sleep <image> 3600`, so the container's
PID 1 exited one hour after start and SIGKILLed the `docker exec` in flight. On a 150 MB download the
lab reported `install.sh exited 137 using only its built-in key` plus two "no OK line" failures -- three
red checks about an installer that had not been given the chance to finish. The ceiling is now
`CHAOS_LAB_KEEPALIVE` (six hours, printed at start so a transcript records the harness's own limit).
The first line of the header below is that print. A green re-run after a harness fix is a different
claim from a green run before it: it says the previous green and the intermediate red describe the
same script.

