**One thing the transcript shows that the green does not cover.** In the section above, the
fetch lines come out in this order: `try:` the origin, then `try:` the mirror, then `from:`
the mirror. The origin was tried, dropped, and the 161,233,728-byte artifact (`content-length`
from `curl -sIL` against that URL today) came from `ghfast.top` instead -- and a run that
succeeds this way says nothing about why origin was skipped. `download_github` collects every
candidate's reason in `reasons`, but prints them only on the `download failed for` path
(`scripts/install.sh:335`-`341`), so the reasons for the candidates that lost are thrown away
the moment one candidate wins. A user who gets a working install from a mirror has no way to
learn that origin was unreachable, or answered with an HTML error page, or truncated -- which
is the same information the 2026-10-03 section above needed and could not get.

The same call site is the one fetch in the installer with no ceiling: `scripts/install.sh:530`
is `download_github "$ORIGIN_URL" "$TMP" 12 0 1048576`, so `max_time` is 0 and `--max-time` is
never added, and no `--speed-limit` is passed either. That is deliberate -- the comment above
it says a 140 MB+ asset must not be cut off mid-transfer -- but with origin-first ordering it
means a candidate that trickles bytes holds the run for as long as it likes, and "downloading
at 3 KB/s" is indistinguishable from "hung". The lab cannot see this either: it waits for the
installer rather than bounding it, and its only ceiling, `CHAOS_LAB_KEEPALIVE`, stops the
container rather than the fetch. Recorded against the installer as an open item rather than
turned into a red here, because every check the lab makes did pass.
